								<div class="title" id="post-title-2543327">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/darkstuff" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1581620); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1581620&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608282638" alt="DarkStuff" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1581620)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/darkstuff" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1581620); return false;" >DarkStuff</a></span> <span class="odate time_1469423548 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">25 Jul 2016 05:12</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2543327">
									

<p>So, here are my thoughts.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Things&nbsp;I&nbsp;Didn't&nbsp;Like</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Or&nbsp;Things&nbsp;that&nbsp;Need&nbsp;to&nbsp;be&nbsp;Changed</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Okay, so&#8230; There were a couple words that were just used&#8230; too much. Tendril was used eight times, and began to get tiring. Synonyms are a wonderful thing, even though sometimes they aren't exactly accurate. I might suggest using some imagery, like &quot;suckerless tentacle&quot; or &quot;whip like protrusion&quot;. I don't know, something to break the monotony of tendril.</p>
<p>Same with gnome, which was used 13 times. This one I don't know how to solve. I guess sometimes you could just call the gnomes &quot;people&quot;, and the reader could infer you are talking about gnomes because that's what this story is about, and what (I think) all the characters are. &quot;Little gnomes&quot; could just be &quot;kids&quot;, for example.</p>
<p>I also found myself getting very tired of glowplant, which was used&#8230; only 4 times? Well, then I don't see why it got on my nerves. But I do remember finding it just a little annoying when Plagum mentioned a glowplant in his mind. I am all for making new sayings to make the world seem stranger and otherworldly (if I remember correctly, a Clockwork Orange did the same thing), but glowplant in my mind is just this world's version of a lightbulb above someone's head. And somehow, that nerved me.</p>
<p>As someone said before, it was hard to identify the connection to the SCP universe, but there was one. Now&#8230; How do you fix this? Easy! You don't even have to change anything about the tale itself. In the post at the top of the discussions, just have a link to the in universe connection. Most people don't have to do this with their tales if the SCP universe connection is obvious, but seeing as this is trying to build a world off of <em>one</em> existing SCP, you might want to point out where it's coming from.</p>
<p>Also, Plagum seemed a bit two dimensional.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Things&nbsp;I&nbsp;Really&nbsp;Enjoyed</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Or&nbsp;Things&nbsp;that&nbsp;Made&nbsp;Me&nbsp;Keep&nbsp;Reading</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I love it when a tale doesn't have to explain itself (I know that slightly contradicts the &quot;tell us where the connection is&quot; part from earlier). I love it when this world is taken for granted, and it <em>is</em> the normal every day. I don't know what the writing tactic is called, but I believe there is a word for when a world or setting is treated as mundane and normal so as to point out the absurd and strange. It's done in a lot of sci-fi, where all the gizmos, gadgets, and light speed convertibles are just normal things, and that makes the world feel more real. Because if we grew up in this world, they would be normal to us, so why make the characters point out how amazing and fantastic it is compared to our world? Love it. Absolutely love it. It works really well here.</p>
<p>&quot;What are sharkworms?&quot; &quot;Oh, y'know, just sharkworms.&quot; (love it)</p>
<p>The description of the festival was great at painting a picture. The description of the gnomes was different than I've ever imagined gnomes (although I am now aware it came from a skip). Unfortunately&#8230; that's about it.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I don't know what to rate this, so I'm going with a neutral vote. The characters seemed a bit 2D, but the world was inventive, but I had no investment into what was going on, but the gnomes were interesting. So, neutral vote. I do want to see more of these tales though, and see if you can improve on the concept, because I really love the concept.</p>

							</div>

								
